<worksheet>
  <title>Forms of Quadratic Functions</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <objectives>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Explore the quadratic function in Standard form.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Explore the quadratic function in Vertex form.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Explore the quadratic function in Factored (Intercept) form.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>
<section>
<title>The Standard form of quadratic functions</title>

<definition xml:id="def-">
    <statement>
        <p>
          A quadratic function is a function that can be represented by an equation of the form <md>f(x)=ax^2+bx+c</md> where <m>a\neq 0</m>. This is called the standard form of a quadratic function.  
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>
<example>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Use Desmos to explore the standard form of a quadratic function.
        </p>
        <figure>

          <interactive desmos="5ksqg0yahd" width="80%" aspect="3:2">
            <shortdescription>An interactive Desmos graph.</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>A Desmos worksheet containing the interactive graph.</p>
            </description>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
    </introduction>

   
        
  

    <task workspace="0.15in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Go to the following <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/5ksqg0yahd">Desmos sheet</url> , or use Desmos withing the worksheet.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.15in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use the sliders to change the values of <m>a, b</m>, and <m>c</m>. Describe how each value affects the graph of the function.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Type <m>(0,c)</m> in the input line to see how the value of <m>c</m> affects the graph. 
            </p>
        </hint>
    </task>
</example>
    </section>

<section>
<title>The Vertex form of quadratic functions</title>   
<definition xml:id="def-vf">
    <statement>
        <p>
           A quadratic function can also be represented by an equation of the form <md>f(x)=a(x-h)^2+k</md> where <m>a\neq 0</m>. This is called the vertex form of a quadratic function. The vertex of the graph of the function is given by the point <m>(h,k)</m>. 
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>

<example>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Use Desmos to explore the vertex form of a quadratic function.
        </p>
        <figure>

          <interactive desmos="6i8y0qmjqb" width="80%" aspect="3:2">
            <shortdescription>An interactive Desmos graph.</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>A Desmos worksheet containing the interactive graph.</p>
            </description>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
    </introduction>

   
        
  

    <task workspace="0.15in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Go to the following <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/6i8y0qmjqb">Desmos sheet</url> , or use Desmos withing the worksheet.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.15in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use the sliders to change the values of <m>a, h</m>, and <m>k</m>. Describe how each value affects the graph of the function.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Type <m>(h,k)</m> in the input line to see how the values of <m>h</m> and <m>k</m> affect the graph. 
            </p>
        </hint>
    </task>
</example>
</section>

<section>
<title>The Factored (Intercept) form of quadratic functions</title>
<definition xml:id="def-ff">
    <statement>
        <p>
           A quadratic function can also be represented by an equation of the form <md>f(x)=a(x-r_1)(x-r_2)</md> where <m>a\neq 0</m>. This is called the factored (or intercept) form of a quadratic function. The x-intercepts of the graph of the function are given by the points <m>(r_1,0)</m> and <m>(r_2,0)</m>.        
           </p>
           </statement>
</definition>
<example>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Use Desmos to explore the factored (intercept) form of a quadratic function.
        </p>
        <figure>    
          <interactive desmos="osaakqwjnd" width="80%" aspect="3:2">
            <shortdescription>An interactive Desmos graph.</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>A Desmos worksheet containing the interactive graph.</p>
            </description>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
    </introduction>   
<task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Go to the following <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/osaakqwjnd">Desmos sheet</url> , or use Desmos withing the worksheet.
        </p>
    </statement>
</task>
<task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Use the sliders to change the values of <m>a, r_1</m>, and <m>r_2</m>. Describe how each value affects the graph of the function.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
        <p>
            Type <m>(r_1,0)</m> and <m>(r_2,0)</m> in the input line to see how the values of <m>r_1</m> and <m>r_2</m> affect the graph.
        </p>
    </hint>
</task>
    </example>      
    </section>
  </worksheet>